Can we use <div> instead of <span>?

Technically yes, the browser will accept it.
But practically and semantically, NO — it is incorrect.

Why <div> should NOT replace <span>:

1) <div> is block-level:
   - Starts on a new line
   - Takes full width
   - Breaks the text flow
   - Disrupts the layout

   Example (bad):
   My favorite color is 
   <div>red</div>
   (This breaks the sentence.)

2) <span> is inline:
   - Stays in the same line
   - Fits naturally inside a sentence
   - Does not break layout

   Example (correct):
   My favorite color is <span>red</span>.

3) <div> is meant for large sections:
   - Grouping big blocks of content
   - Creating layout structures
   - Wrapping containers

   It is NOT meant for styling a single word or phrase.

4) Using <div> for small inline text creates messy HTML
   → This is called "div soup".

Correct Usage Summary:
- To style a word/phrase → use <span>.
- To group large sections → use <div>.
- For layouts/containers → use <div>.
- For highlighting inline text → use <span>.
